            "url": "https://www.livescience.com/health/psychology/what-is-the-science-behind-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Scientists suggest that d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu episodes may arise from seizures in the brain's temporal lobe or dysfunction in brain regions involved in storing and retrieving memory, like the hippocampus and parahippocampus.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udca1 Leading Theories\n\nThere are many competing explanations. Here are the most prominent:\n\n1. **The Brain's Fact-Checking Theory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "According to O'Connor, d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs when brain areas, such as the temporal lobe, send signals to the frontal decision-making regions, indicating tha...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDAjM+D5vf22dWtYAShoMxBEu0y0x2SOmVYX5IjBFz0RgIH8w35NkCNMeUQq2sDBjz77w9TRKp5SxirbZOvv08M8+nDHFIgwpYbpRSxgqFdjSKIRZ4PPQu8ojqmyQJEVVFgWsuxgE",
            "title": "The Fascinating Science of D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu | Psychology Today",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/brain-reboot/202312/the-fascinating-science-of-deja-vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to researcher Akira O'Connor, d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs when brain areas such as the temporal lobe send signals to the frontal decision-making regions, indicating that an experience is repeating itself. The frontal regions then evaluate the consistency of this signal with past experiences. If there is no prior experience, the realization of d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu occurs.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n2. **The Attentional Theory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "The four most prevalent theories are: Attentional: suggests when our attention is divided, we take in environmental information subconsciously, so tha...",
            "encrypted_index": "Eo8BCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDKFbljT8sBkzLsMdXBoMZR6jgnpIJfabNG56IjDQYc6z3ud9YElN92f4sgw1dpse2uZpZHnQRLHTRs3WT5fxKjHNnxsoKB3g+9AKPq8qE4W4YGjmxpJZs49dHJL3Sw5QObkYBA==",
            "title": "D\u00e9j\u00e0 Vu: The neuroscience behind the mysterious phenomenon",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.inspirethemind.org/post/d%C3%A9j%C3%A0-vu-the-neuroscience-behind-the-mysterious-phenomenon"
          }
        ],
        "text": "This theory suggests that when our attention is divided, we take in environmental information subconsciously, so that when we return to full awareness and pay attention, we feel we have seen it all before \u2014 resulting in d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n3. **The Dual-Processing / Signal Delay Theory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "In 1965, Robert Efron of Boston's Veterans Hospital proposed that d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is caused by dual neurological processing caused by delayed signals. Efron f...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpIBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDIxTEZDKFIweRFPYDRoMRyA95g9aRCQGZz7NIjCB8KAM5yfEmYXN2sNsvhJPf9G4H570tP4pJMhYokvH9Flh8EYBma/bbAgjMQ6BB+0qFlHHyzdBqKRk+48K2ltA5h4ExqwDIz4YBA==",
            "title": "D\u00e9j\u00e0 vu - Wikipedia",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A9j%C3%A0_vu"
          }
        ],
        "text": "In 1965, Robert Efron proposed that d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu is caused by dual neurological processing caused by delayed signals. The brain's sorting of incoming signals is done in the temporal lobe of the left hemisphere. However, signals enter the temporal lobe twice \u2014 once from each hemisphere \u2014 normally with a slight delay of milliseconds between them. Efron proposed that if the two signals were occasionally not synchronized properly, they would be processed as two separate experiences, with the second seeming to be a re-living of the first.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "\n\n4. **The Pattern-Matching Theory:** ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "Walking into a room would be the cue that triggers an involuntary retrieval of memory; there\u2019d be no \u201caccess to content,\u201d meaning there\u2019s no memory to...",
            "encrypted_index": "Eo8BCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDBGpR2H31TF7nTVoXxoMWrgequqpbqacZ8LKIjCd5vHrNM+bHuT6CiXv+B7SQKYAo1wDHdxlTccTLe23t1Q25/8TUx/hE1WA4jkFovEqE0tQIgV22YoaBY9MU+nAi65m/ZgYBA==",
            "title": "How does science explain d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu? It's a brain glitch with a purpose. | Popular Science",
            "type": "web_search_result_location",
            "url": "https://www.popsci.com/science/deja-vu-science/"
          }
        ],
        "text": "Walking into a room would be the cue that triggers an involuntary retrieval of memory; there'd be no \"access to content\" \u2014 no actual memory to compare \u2014 but the feeling of familiarity is there anyway.",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": " ",
        "type": "text"
      },
      {
        "citations": [
          {
            "cited_text": "One small study examining d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu used immersive virtual reality to create standard everyday scenes\u2014such as a bowling alley or a garden\u2014which were sho...",
            "encrypted_index": "EpEBCioIEBgCIiRkMmJjZWZjOS0wNDQwLTQxYWUtOTM0Mi1hNDFhNjdkYWIzZWQSDFBt/oDxb2gpAPfUxRoMwUU4KgxB4YtBVbjJIjDMcv2ZCUVYqdIK04ydJb6DoQFgDZ5IkrpyyyKkA/nQcLLLdB0HSP/VRmukVe821Q4qFahbxYB2LU04dl7g2AG1zz4I/+NgABgE",
